# The preset policy in kgsm-base decides whether the bot runs on a node; applying it is symlink
# manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. Starting it is the
# node's post-transaction hook's job — and that hook will NOT start it until the token below is set,
# because a bot with no token connects to nothing.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who disabled this unit keeps it disabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly undo that.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-bot.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-bot is enabled and waiting on one value:

    Discord__Token  in  /etc/kgsm-bot/kgsm-bot.env

  Set it, then start the service:

    systemctl start kgsm-bot.service

  Nothing in configuration names a Discord server. A guild hears about this host because an admin
  ran /setup there, which needs a KGSM admin account — inviting the bot somewhere grants that guild
  nothing on its own.

  The conversational half is a client of kgsm-llm. Without it, slash commands, announcements and the
  status board all work; @-mentioning the bot says so and goes quiet.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-bot/kgsm-bot.env, merge it — new settings are declared
  there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}
